HEMP SEED GERMINATION + NITRATE/GA3 COMPARISON
===============================================

PURPOSE
-------
Use separate treatments to determine whether an older seed lot responds
better to ordinary hydration, a nitrate germination signal, or gibberellic
acid (GA3).

IMPORTANT
---------
These are experimental screening concentrations, not guaranteed hemp-specific
optima. Old seeds may fail because the embryo is no longer viable. Neither
nitrate nor GA3 can restore a dead embryo.

Do not initially combine GA3 + nitrate. Separate treatments make it possible
to determine which treatment, if any, improves germination.


===============================================================
A. CONTROL
===============================================================

Treatment:
    Clean water only

Purpose:
    Establish the untreated germination baseline.

Example:
    10-25 seeds

Soak:
    Approximately 8-12 hours

Then:
    Transfer to a moist, aerated germination medium.

Avoid:
    Completely waterlogged conditions.

Record:
    - number germinated
    - time to radicle emergence
    - abnormal seedlings
    - mold/decay
    - normal established seedlings


===============================================================
B. POTASSIUM NITRATE (KNO3) COMPARISON
===============================================================

Experimental comparison series:

    CONTROL      = 0 g/L
    VERY LOW     = 0.5 g/L KNO3  = 0.05%
    LOW          = 1.0 g/L KNO3  = 0.10%
    MODERATE     = 2.0 g/L KNO3  = 0.20%

Example preparation:

                 100 mL     500 mL     1 L
    -------------------------------------------------
    0.05% KNO3   0.05 g     0.25 g      0.50 g
    0.10% KNO3   0.10 g     0.50 g      1.00 g
    0.20% KNO3   0.20 g     1.00 g      2.00 g


Suggested screening design:

    Group A = water control
    Group B = 0.05% KNO3
    Group C = 0.10% KNO3
    Group D = 0.20% KNO3

Use the same:
    - seed lot
    - seed count
    - temperature
    - germination medium
    - moisture
    - observation schedule

Do NOT assume:

    more nitrate = more germination

A better conceptual response is:

                    optimal region
                         /\
                        /  \
                       /    \
                      /      \
    germination _____/        \_____ stress/inhibition
                 low nitrate          excessive nitrate


===============================================================
C. GIBBERELLIC ACID (GA3) COMPARISON
===============================================================

Experimental screening concentrations:

    CONTROL   =   0 ppm
    LOW       =  25 ppm
    LOW-MOD   =  50 ppm
    MODERATE  = 100 ppm
    HIGH TEST = 200 ppm

Example quantities of PURE GA3:

                    100 mL     500 mL     1 L
    ------------------------------------------------
     25 ppm          2.5 mg     12.5 mg      25 mg
     50 ppm          5.0 mg     25.0 mg      50 mg
    100 ppm         10.0 mg     50.0 mg     100 mg
    200 ppm         20.0 mg    100.0 mg     200 mg


CAUTION:

Commercial GA3 products may NOT be 100% active ingredient.

Therefore:

required product mass =
    desired GA3 mass / active-ingredient fraction


Example:

If a product contained 20% active GA3:

active_fraction = 0.20

To obtain 50 mg active GA3:

product_mass =
    50 mg / 0.20
    =
    250 mg product

Follow the manufacturer's mixing directions because GA3 formulations differ.


===============================================================
D. OLD-SEED SCREENING EXPERIMENT
===============================================================

Preferably use at least:

    10-25 seeds per treatment

Example experimental groups:

    GROUP 1:
        Water control

    GROUP 2:
        0.10% KNO3

    GROUP 3:
        0.20% KNO3

    GROUP 4:
        50 ppm GA3

    GROUP 5:
        100 ppm GA3


Keep every other condition identical.


Suggested observations:

DAY / TIME
    |
    +--> seed hydrated?
    |
    +--> seed coat opened?
    |
    +--> radicle emerged?
    |
    +--> normal root development?
    |
    +--> normal shoot development?
    |
    +--> decay/mold?
    |
    +--> normal established seedling?


===============================================================
E. DO NOT COUNT "SPLITTING" ALONE AS SUCCESS
===============================================================

Use:

SEED
  |
  v
HYDRATION
  |
  v
GERMINATION ACTIVATION
  |
  v
COAT SPLITTING
  |
  v
RADICLE EMERGENCE
  |
  v
ROOT + SHOOT DEVELOPMENT
  |
  v
NORMAL SEEDLING


A seed that cracks open but subsequently dies should NOT be counted
as a successful normal germination.


Recommended primary measurement:

normal_germination_percent =
    100 * normal_seedlings / total_seeds


Example:

20 seeds tested
14 coat splits
12 radicles
10 normal seedlings

coat_split_percent =
    14/20 * 100
    =
    70%

radicle_percent =
    12/20 * 100
    =
    60%

normal_germination_percent =
    10/20 * 100
    =
    50%


